L483: **BMJ Open**
L484: **ABSTRACT**
L485: **Objectives**
L486: In older adults there is a blunted responsiveness to resistance training and reduced muscle hypertrophy
L487: compared with younger adults. There is evidence that both exercise training and vitamin D supplementation
L488: may benefit musculoskeletal health in older adults, and it is plausible that in combination their effects may be
L489: additive. The aim of this systematic review was to evaluate the effectiveness of combined resistance exercise
L490: training and vitamin D3 supplementation on musculoskeletal health in older adults.
L491: **Data sources**
L492: A comprehensive search of electronic databases, including Science Direct, MedLine, PubMed, Google Scholar
L493: and Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials (Cochrane CENTRAL accessed by Wiley Science) **.**
L494: **Results**
L495: 7 studies were included, with a total of 792 participants aged 65 years or over (or mean age ≥65 years). Studies
L496: were categorized into two groups; group 1 compared vitamin D3 supplementation and exercise training versus
L497: exercise alone, group 2 compared vitamin D3 supplementation and exercise training versus vitamin D3
L498: supplementation alone. Meta-analyses for group 1 found muscle strength of the lower limb to be significantly
L499: improved within the intervention group (0.98, 95% CI 0.73, 1.24, _p_ <0.001); all other outcomes showed small
L500: but non-significant positive effects for the intervention group. The SPPB, TUG, muscle strength of the lower
L501: limb and hip BMD all showed significantly greater improvements in the intervention group for group 2
L502: comparisons.
L503: **Conclusions**
L504: This review provides tentative support for the additive effect of resistance exercise and vitamin D3
L505: supplementation for the improvement of muscle strength in older adults. For other aspects of musculoskeletal
L506: function, such as SPPB and TUG, no additional benefit beyond exercise was shown. Further evidence is
L507: required to draw firm conclusions or make explicit recommendations regarding combined exercise and vitamin
L508: D3 supplementation.
L509: **Strengths and Limitations of this study**
L510:   - To the best of our knowledge this study represents the first review evaluating the combined effects of
L511: vitamin D3 supplementation and exercise in older adults
L512:   - Generally, outcome measure data could be graded as representing moderate quality
L513:   - Only seven studies were found to be eligible for inclusion, highlighting the lack of literature available
L514: on the topic
L515:   - The inclusion of one high risk study was deemed necessary due to the lack of eligible studies
L516: 2
