L1085: **BMJ Open**
L1086: **Outcome measures**
L1087: All outcomes are listed in Table 3. Group 1 studies had few outcomes in common; however, all measured
L1088: muscle strength[21,31,32]; isometric knee extensor strength was measured using a strain gauge[21,31] and
L1089: isometric quadriceps strength was measured using a quadriceps table[32]. Hand grip strength was measured
L1090: using a hand grip dynamometer[32]. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) was used to measure the CSA of the
L1091: quadriceps[31,37], whilst[32] analysed fat and lean mass using dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA). 2
L1092: studies measured timed-up and go (TUG), hip and spine bone mineral density (BMD)[21,32]. 1 study analysed
L1093: fibre type and muscle quality[31].
L1094: Of group 2 studies,[21,32,34,36] assessed lower limb strength, and[32,35] measured grip strength. Muscle
L1095: power was measured as sit-to-stand transfer power[33] and the stair climb test[34]. The short physical
L1096: performance battery (SPPB) was assessed by[32,34], and the TUG by[21,32,34]. BMD of the hip[21,32,34-36]
L1097: and spine[21,32,34,35] were measured using DXA. Lean mass was measured using DXA[32-34]and X-ray
L1098: computed tomography (CT)[36]. Balance was assessed via the Romberg ratio[32], four square step test[34], an
L1099: AccuSway platform[35] and backwards walking[21]. Other outcomes included endurance (12-minute
L1100: walk[32]), the 30 second sit-to-stand test[34], normal walking speed and the 5-time chair stand test[21].
