L1319: **BMJ Open**
L1320: Group 2 comparisons included the SPPB, TUG, muscle strength of the lower limb, hand grip strength, weight,
L1321: lean mass, fat mass and the BMD of the hip and spine (Figures 6-14). Of these outcomes, hand grip strength,
L1322: weight, lean mass, fat mass and the BMD of the spine were found to be non-significant. However, SPPB score
L1323: was more improved in the intervention group (1.09, 95% CI 0.15, 2.03. _p_ = 0.02), with a significant and large
L1324: effect. Similarly, TUG was significantly reduced within the intervention group (-1.57, 95% CI -2.50, -0.64. _p_ =
L1325: 0.0010). The results of the quantitative analysis also supported the combined intervention for muscle strength
L1326: of the lower limb (2.69, 95% CI 0.95, 4.42). _p_ = 0.002), and BMD of the hip (0.04, 95% CI 0.01, 0.06. _p_ = 0.002).
L1327: **Qualitative synthesis**
L1328: Referring to the narrative synthesis guidelines provided by the Cochrane Consumers and Communication
L1329: Review Group[38], it was appropriate to apply 2 steps listed; developing a preliminary synthesis and exploring
L1330: the relationships within and between studies. To develop a primary synthesis, results were systematically
L1331: tabulated to identify patterns across studies (Tables 7-9). Exploring the relationships between and within
L1332: studies for group 1, the control group in study[31]demonstrated a significant percentage increase in CSA of the
L1333: quadriceps from baseline in comparison to the intervention group (+8.46% versus +4.94%, _p_ < 0.05).
L1334: Comparing primary outcomes for group 2, the percentage increase in isometric knee extensor strength for
L1335: study[36] was greater in the intervention group (+3.01% versus +0.11%), although not statistically significant.
L1336: Muscle power was compared in studies[33] and[34], expressed as sit-to-stand transfer power and functional
L1337: stair climbing muscle power respectively. Both studies reported a significant percentage increase in muscle
L1338: power within the intervention groups, and smaller, non-significant increases within the control groups (sit-to
L1339: stand transfer power intervention group +8.00% versus +2.61%, _p_ = 0.017; functional stair climbing muscle
L1340: power intervention group +10.51% versus +7.32%, _p_ < 0.05).
L1341: The 30 second sit-to-stand test showed significant favourable results for the combined intervention of exercise
L1342: and vitamin D3 (+10.40% versus +6.20%, p<0.05). Although normal walking speed, 5-time chair stand time and
L1343: the 12-minute walk test were further improved within the control groups, this did not achieve statistical
L1344: significance. The four square step test, body sway and backwards walking were significantly more improved in
L1345: the intervention groups. Only Romberg ratio showed the greatest improvement within the control group;
L1346: Romberg ratio was decreased in comparison with the intervention group, although the results were non
L1347: significant (+2.8% versus -0.60%).
L1348: For group 2 secondary outcomes, small and non-significant gains in appendicular lean mass were
L1349: demonstrated in the intervention group of study[33]. In study[36], muscle mass of the upper limb decreased
L1350: non-significantly in both the intervention and control groups, although to a lesser extent in the intervention
L1351: group. BMD of the hip was gained in both groups, although by a higher percentage in the control group; both
L1352: trends were non-significant.
