L1416: **BMJ Open**
L1417: In summary, meta-analyses for group 1 found muscle strength of the lower limb to be significantly improved
L1418: within the intervention group (0.98, 95% CI 0.73, 1.24, _p_ <0.001). All other outcomes showed small but non
L1419: significant positive effects for the intervention group. The SPPB, TUG, muscle strength of the lower limb and
L1420: hip BMD all showed significantly greater improvements in the intervention group for group 2 comparisons.
L1421: The narrative analysis revealed significant differences in body composition, muscle power, muscle function
L1422: and balance. A significant percentage increase in quadriceps CSA was observed in the control group of
L1423: study[31]. The combined intervention of RET and vitamin D3 supplementation resulted in a greater percentage
L1424: increase in muscle strength and power, and a greater improvement in the 30 second sit-to-stand test, the four
L1425: square step test, body sway and backwards walking. However, vitamin D3 supplementation alone resulted in a
L1426: greater improvement in normal walking speed, 5-time chair stand time, the 12-minute walk test and Romberg
L1427: ratio.
L1428: **DISCUSSION**
L1429: The aim of this systematic review was to assess the combined effect of RET and vitamin D3 supplementation
L1430: on musculoskeletal health in older adults. Only 7 studies were eligible for inclusion, with a total of 792
L1431: participants, highlighting the lack of available literature on the topic. Studies were categorised into 2 groups;
L1432: studies in which all participants took part in RET and the intervention group was supplemented with vitamin
L1433: D3, or studies in which all participants were supplemented with vitamin D3 and the intervention group took
L1434: part in RET. 2 studies were categorized into both group 1 and group 2.
L1435: **Quantitative analysis**
L1436: Data analysis conducted for this review included meta-analyses and narrative reviews. Meta-analyses for
L1437: group 1 included muscle strength of the lower limb, TUG and BMD of both the hip and spine. Evidence of
L1438: additional benefit was shown for all outcomes within the intervention group; however, the effect size was
L1439: small and non-significant for TUG and BMD of the hip and spine. Muscle strength of the lower limb was the
L1440: only significant outcome of group 1, with a large effect size observed within the intervention group (0.98, 95%
L1441: CI 0.73, 1.24. _p_ <0.00001). Although numerous studies have demonstrated the beneficial effect of RET on
L1442: muscle strength in older adults[3-5], this result provides evidence that vitamin D3 supplementation may
L1443: enhance these effects in older adults. Skeletal muscle myopathies associated with vitamin D deficiency are
L1444: well documented[39], and symptoms of significant muscle weakness are reversed with treatment of the
L1445: deficiency[40]. A systematic review and meta-analysis reported a gain in lower extremity strength with vitamin
L1446: D supplementation only in vitamin D deficient older adults; no effect was observed in replete adults[22].
L1447: Similarly, no effect of vitamin D3 supplementation on isometric quadriceps strength was demonstrated after 6
L1448: months in vitamin D replete older adults[41]. Interestingly, although the studies included within group
L1449: 1[21,31,32] did not specify serum 25(OH)D levels as inclusion/exclusion criteria, baseline and post-intervention
L1450: serum 25(OH)D were within the ‘sufficient’ range (>30nmol.L [-1] ). A greater increase of muscle strength in
