L1514: **BMJ Open**
L1515: replete older adults represents a novel finding of this review. Preliminary support for combined vitamin D
L1516: supplementation and RET was demonstrated in a 3 month longitudinal study examining the effect of serum
L1517: 25(OH)D and exercise training on functional performance in older men and women aged 65 years and over. No
L1518: significant improvements in function were reported in participants with lower serum 25(OH)D (<47.5 nmol.L [-1] ),
L1519: however higher serum 25(OH)D (>67.5 nmol.L [-1] ) was associated with greatest improvements in functionality
L1520: and muscle strength[42].
L1521: This finding must be considered within the context of the risk of bias and GRADE analyses. The risk of bias
L1522: analysis showed an overall unclear risk of bias for the included studies, and the GRADE analysis concluded that
L1523: the evidenced was of moderate quality; however, serious inconsistency due to moderate heterogeneity (I [2] =
L1524: 70%) was detected. This heterogeneity may have been due to the differing duration of interventions (12 weeks
L1525: to 24 months), differences between measurement methodologies, differences between exercise regimens
L1526: (although all adopted progressive RET), doses of vitamin D3 (400 IU to 1920 IU per day), or may indicate that
L1527: these studies were unsuitable for comparison.
L1528: Significant effects for the SPPB, TUG, muscle strength of the lower limb, and the BMD of the hip were observed
L1529: within the intervention groups of group 2 studies; unsurprisingly, RET was found to have a positive influence.
L1530: In a recent systematic review and meta-analysis, exercise significantly increased SPPB score and decreased
L1531: TUG time, with large effect sizes (1.87 and -2.47 respectively[43]); similar results are reported within this
L1532: review. Vitamin D is a regulator of BMD, proliferating calcium and phosphate absorption in the intestine and
L1533: acting directly on bone cells[44]. Vitamin D has previously been shown to influence BMD, fracture rate and
L1534: risk[45]; studies of patients who have sustained a hip fracture typically demonstrated low serum vitamin D
L1535: (≤30.0 nmol.L [-1] ;[46]). Supplementation of vitamin D and calcium has been shown to significantly decrease the
L1536: rate of bone loss in the hip and spine[47]. GRADE analyses for these outcomes concluded the quality of
L1537: evidence to be high (SPPB and TUG) or moderate (muscle strength of the lower limb and BMD of the hip).
L1538: Closer examination of the control groups within significant outcomes for group 2 was undertaken to evaluate
L1539: the effect of vitamin D3 supplementation alone. Intriguingly, although the intervention groups (RET and
L1540: vitamin D3 supplementation) showed evidence of benefit in number of outcomes, the control groups (vitamin
L1541: D3 supplementation alone) showed mixed, or even negative impacts on the same outcomes. SPPB score was
L1542: decreased post-intervention compared with baseline by 0.30% and 0.50% in the control groups of studies[32]
L1543: and[33] respectively. Muscle strength of the lower limb and BMD of the hip showed mixed results for the
L1544: intervention groups, with some studies reporting small increases and others reporting small losses (non
L1545: significant). Previous reports of the effect of vitamin D supplementation on muscle strength and physical
L1546: functioning are mixed; the InCHIANTI study of people aged 65 years or over reported a significant association
L1547: between serum 25(OH)D <25nmol.L [-1] and SPPB score[48]. Similarly, a large prospective cohort of older adults
L1548: aged 65 years or over found those with low (<25nmol.L [-1] ) 25(OH)D were significantly more likely to experience
L1549: losses in grip strength and higher rates of appendicular lean mass loss compared to those with higher (>50
L1550: nmol.L [-1] ) 25(OH)D[23]. Conversely, another large, prospective study found no association between serum
