L1614: **BMJ Open**
L1615: 25(OH)D, walking speed and time for repeated chair stands[49]. The TUG test time was actually significantly
L1616: increased within the control group of study[32], and increased by a smaller, non-significant amount in
L1617: study[21]. Again, participants included in studies[32] and [21] had sufficient serum 25(OH)D levels, indicating
L1618: that supplementation in replete older adults may not confer additional benefits to neuromuscular function
L1619: unless combined with exercise.
L1620: **Narrative analysis**
L1621: Studies in group 1[21,31,32] had few body composition outcomes in common, therefore a narrative analysis
L1622: was conducted. The CSA of the quadriceps was analysed within study[31], and results showed that although
L1623: the intervention group did experience a +4.94%, increase from baseline, the control group (not supplemented
L1624: with vitamin D3) actually showed a significantly higher increase in quadriceps CSA (+8.46%, _p_ <0.05).
L1625: These results do not provide evidence for the additive effects of combined exercise training and vitamin D3.
L1626: Other study groups have reported changes in muscle CSA consequent to RET which are both smaller[8,50] and
L1627: comparable[51] to those reported in study[31]. Interestingly, study[31] also assessed “muscle quality” (muscle
L1628: strength/CSA); although non-significant, the intervention group improved their muscle quality to a greater
L1629: degree than the control group (+9.61% versus +0.66% change from baseline). The intervention and control
L1630: groups both increased their muscle strength to a similar degree, and there was no significant difference
L1631: between these changes; however, the control group (as previously mentioned) demonstrated a larger increase
L1632: in their muscle CSA. This shows that the gains in muscle strength in the intervention group surpassed the
L1633: improvements made in muscle CSA, indicative of an increased functionality of the muscle to produce force;
L1634: conceptually more relevant in combatting the effects of sarcopenia than muscle size and strength alone[52].
L1635: Results of the narrative analysis for group 2 showed that the combined intervention of RET and vitamin D3
L1636: supplementation was significantly more beneficial than vitamin D3 supplementation alone for sit-to-stand
L1637: transfer power, functional stair climbing muscle power, 30 second sit-to-stand, 5-time chair stand, the four
L1638: square step test, body sway and backwards walking. The control groups also showed benefits although to a
L1639: lesser degree; the only significant improvement for the control group was for the TUG in study[32] ( _p_ =0.0006).
L1640: Only body sway was negatively affected by vitamin D3 supplementation, although the within group change
L1641: was non-significant. Other outcomes of interest included normal walking speed, the distance walked in 12
L1642: minutes and Romberg ratio, in which the control groups made the most improvement, although not
L1643: significantly.
L1644: **Limitations**
L1645: Few published studies were eligible for inclusion within this review, although this serves to highlight the
L1646: knowledge gap with respect to this topic. The inclusion of a high risk study was deemed necessary due to the
L1647: lack of available literature, although this had a negative effect on the perceived quality of evidence for the
L1648: outcomes in which it was reported. Generally, outcome measure data could be graded as representing
