L1712: **BMJ Open**
L1713: moderate quality, although there were several outcome measures graded as low or very low quality, due to
L1714: the high variability of participant numbers, duration of interventions, exercise methodologies or differing
L1715: vitamin D3 doses and period of supplementation employed within the studies. Furthermore, data produced
L1716: from meta-analyses including study[21] may have been skewed due to the high weighting assigned for this
L1717: study as a result of the large number of participants recruited.
L1718: Of the individual studies included within this review, none reported inclusion/exclusion criterion for vitamin D
L1719: status, and although at baseline serum vitamin D was not significantly different between the groups in 5
L1720: studies[21,31-33,36], 2 studies reported no data for serum vitamin D pre or post-intervention[34,35].
L1721: Additionally, analysis methods used within 5 studies included did not account for confounding factors[31
L1722: 34,36], and participants were not stratified on the basis of any characteristics in 3 studies[21,31,35], although
L1723: these were single-sex studies. Unfortunately, several outcome measures were unsuitable for inclusion within
L1724: the qualitative analysis due to differing measurement methodologies utilised or too few outcome measures in
L1725: common.
L1726: **CONCLUSION**
L1727: This review provides tentative support for the additive effect of combined RET and vitamin D3
L1728: supplementation for the improvement of muscle strength in older adults. For other aspects of musculoskeletal
L1729: function, such as SPPB and TUG, no additional benefit beyond that gained from exercise training was found.
L1730: This review showed no evidence of benefit of vitamin D3 supplementation alone, however, few studies were
L1731: identified during the literature search, highlighting that further evidence is required to draw any firm
L1732: conclusions or make explicit recommendations regarding vitamin D3 supplementation for musculoskeletal
L1733: health and function in older adults.
