L3262: **BMJ Open**
L3263: **ABSTRACT**
L3264: **Objectives**
L3265: In older adults there is a blunted responsiveness to resistance training and reduced muscle hypertrophy
L3266: compared with younger adults. There is evidence that both exercise training and vitamin D supplementation
L3267: may benefit musculoskeletal health in older adults, and it is plausible that in combination their effects may be
L3268: additive. The aim of this systematic review was to evaluate the effectiveness of combined resistance exercise
L3269: training and vitamin D3 supplementation on musculoskeletal health in older adults.
L3270: **Data sources**
L3271: A comprehensive search of electronic databases, including Science Direct, MedLine, PubMed, Google Scholar
L3272: and Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials (Cochrane CENTRAL accessed by Wiley Science). Eligible
L3273: studies were randomized controlled trials including men and women (aged ≥65 years or mean age ≥ 65 years);
L3274: enlisting resistance exercise training (RET) and vitamin D3 supplementation; including outcomes of muscle
L3275: strength, function, muscle power, body composition, serum vitamin D/calcium status or quality of life (4)
L3276: comparing results with a control group. The review was informed by a pre-registered protocol
L3277: (http://www.crd.york.ac.uk/PROSPERO/display_record.asp?ID=CRD42015020157).
L3278: **Results**
L3279: 7 studies including a total of 792 participants were identified. Studies were categorized into two groups; group
L3280: 1 compared vitamin D3 supplementation and exercise training versus exercise alone (describing the additive
L3281: effect of vitamin D3 supplementation when combined with resistance exercise training) and group 2 compared
L3282: vitamin D3 supplementation and exercise training versus vitamin D3 supplementation alone (describing the
L3283: additive effect of resistance exercise training when combined with vitamin D3 supplementation).
L3284: Meta-analyses for group 1 found muscle strength of the lower limb to be significantly improved within the
L3285: intervention group (0.98, 95% CI 0.73, 1.24, _p_ <0.001); all other outcomes showed small but non-significant
L3286: positive effects for the intervention group. The Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB), Timed Up and Go
L3287: (TUG), muscle strength of the lower limb and femoral neck Bone Mineral Density (BMD) showed significantly
L3288: greater improvements in the intervention group for group 2 comparisons.
L3289: **Conclusions**
L3290: This review provides tentative support for the additive effect of resistance exercise and vitamin D3
L3291: supplementation for the improvement of muscle strength in older adults. For other functional variables, such
L3292: as SPPB and TUG, no additional benefit beyond exercise was shown. Further evidence is required to draw firm
L3293: conclusions or make explicit recommendations regarding combined exercise and vitamin D3 supplementation.
L3294: **Strengths and Limitations of this study**
L3295:   - To the best of our knowledge this study represents the first review evaluating the combined effects of
L3296: vitamin D3 supplementation and exercise in older adults
L3297:   - Generally, outcome measure data could be graded as representing moderate quality
L3298:   - Only seven studies were found to be eligible for inclusion, highlighting the lack of literature available
L3299: on the topic
L3300:   - The inclusion of one high risk study was deemed necessary due to the lack of eligible studies
L3301: 2
