L3364: **BMJ Open**
L3365: **INTRODUCTION**
L3366: Sarcopenia, originally defined as the age related loss of muscle mass[1], now also encompasses low muscle
L3367: strength and/or muscle function[2]. The efficacy of resistance training in preventing or alleviating age-related
L3368: musculoskeletal loss is well established; cited as the most promising intervention for improving symptoms of
L3369: sarcopenia[3].
L3370: Clear evidence exists demonstrating an association between resistance exercise training (RET) and muscle
L3371: hypertrophy, which is maintained in older age[3-5]. However, in older adults there is a blunted responsiveness
L3372: to RET in comparison with younger adults; a blunted muscle protein synthetic rate in response to a single bout
L3373: of resistance exercise has been reported[6], and others demonstrate a reduction in muscle hypertrophy in
L3374: comparison to younger adults[7-10]. This ’anabolic resistance’ may be due to changes in gene expression and
L3375: anabolic signalling; an attenuated anabolic hormone response to resistance exercise is observed in comparison
L3376: to younger adults[11].
L3377: Losses in muscle strength are associated with losses in functional ability, independence and increases in frailty,
L3378: falls, and disability in older adults [12-15]; therefore, there may be merit associated with a combination of
L3379: interventions to boost responsiveness of older muscle to resistance exercise and combat anabolic resistance.
L3380: Vitamin D3 supplementation in humans has been shown to positively influence musculoskeletal health in older
L3381: adults: increases in relative number and cross-sectional area (CSA) of muscle fibres (type II in particular) has
L3382: been reported[16-18], and muscle strength increased and fall rates decreased after treatment with vitamin
L3383: D3[17]. Vitamin D receptor (VDR) concentration significantly increased with vitamin D3 supplementation[18];
L3384: conversely, supplementation conferred no benefits on strength, functioning and balance[19-21]. Moreover, a
L3385: systematic review examining the effects of vitamin D3 supplementation in vitamin D replete adults aged over
L3386: 18 years found no significant effect on grip or proximal lower limb muscle strength; however, pooled data
L3387: including vitamin D deficient participants (serum 25(OH)D <25 nmol.l [-1] ) demonstrated a large effect on hip
L3388: muscle strength[22].
L3389: There is conflicting evidence surrounding the efficacy of vitamin D3 supplementation alone or in combination
L3390: with exercise on musculoskeletal health, with no clear consensus regarding the management or prevention of
L3391: sarcopenia. Although epidemiological data suggest a relationship between vitamin D3 and muscle
L3392: weakness[23], this association is not well understood, and evidence in published literature is lacking and
L3393: contradictory. Considering the beneficial effects of both RET and vitamin D3 on muscle tissue, it is plausible an
L3394: additive effect would exist if combined, optimizing the potential for healthy ageing muscle[24]. Thus, the aim
L3395: of this study was to assess the combined effect of RET and vitamin D3 supplementation on musculoskeletal
L3396: health in older adults.
L3397: 3
