L3642: **BMJ Open**
L3643: **Data items and collection**
L3644: Data were extracted independently by 2 reviewers (AEA and ASA) using a standardised data extraction sheet;
L3645: any disagreements were discussed and resolved with a third person (CAG). The inter-rater reliability assessed
L3646: using Cohen’s Kappa, was found to be excellent (86% agreement)[27]. Data items including general
L3647: information, participant characteristics and details of the intervention were extracted. For key outcomes, the
L3648: definition used by the authors, methodology, results, mean differences and the presence/absence of statistical
L3649: significance were reported.
L3650: **Risk of bias analysis**
L3651: 2 reviewers (AEA and CAG) independently assessed the validity of included studies, with provisions for
L3652: moderation from a third reviewer. The Cochrane Collaboration’s tool for assessing risk of bias was utilised, as
L3653: described in the Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions[25]; the use of scales for
L3654: assessment is explicitly discouraged[28,29]. Pre-specified consensus points were devised and agreed by
L3655: reviewers to ensure consistency. It was acknowledged that by nature of design, blinding of participants and
L3656: personnel would be difficult in certain studies; therefore grading was based on the likelihood that outcome
L3657: measures were influenced by the potential lack of blinding[25].
L3658: **Grading the quality of evidence**
L3659: The Grading of Recommendation, Assessment, Development, and Evaluation (GRADE) handbook[30] was used
L3660: to evaluate the quality of evidence of outcomes assessed within the meta-analyses. The GRADE approach
L3661: utilises systematically produced questions to reach conclusions on degree of confidence in the estimate of the
L3662: effect. GRADE assesses patient important outcomes across five areas; risk of bias, inconsistency, indirectness,
L3663: imprecision and publication bias, and grades outcomes as demonstrating high, moderate, low or very low
L3664: quality of evidence.
L3665: **RESULTS**
L3666: **Study selection:**
L3667: 7 studies were included within the review; Agergaard et al., 2015[31], Bunout et al., 2006[32], Drey et al.,
L3668: 2011[33], Gianoudis et al., 2014[34], Jessup et al., 2003[35], Uusi-Rasi et al., 2015[21], and Verschueren et al.,
L3669: 2011[36]; the study flow diagram is presented in Figure 1.
L3670: Upon reading full text articles, it became clear that there were 2 separate groups of interventions; group 1, in
L3671: which all participants took part in RET and the intervention arm was supplemented with vitamin D3 (describing
L3672: the additive effect of vitamin D3 supplementation when combined with resistance exercise training), group 2
L3673: in which all participants were supplemented with vitamin D3 and the intervention arm took part in RET
L3674: (describing the additive effect of resistance exercise training when combined with vitamin D3
L3675: supplementation); and studies using a combination of the 2 interventions (Table 2).
