L3908: **BMJ Open**
L3909: **Outcome measures**
L3910: All outcomes are listed in Table 3. Group 1 studies had few outcomes in common; however, all measured
L3911: muscle strength[21,31,32]; isometric knee extensor strength was measured using a strain gauge[21,31] and
L3912: isometric quadriceps strength was measured using a quadriceps table[32]. Hand grip strength was measured
L3913: using a hand grip dynamometer[32]. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) was used to measure the CSA of the
L3914: quadriceps[31,37], whilst[32] analysed fat and lean mass using dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA). 2
L3915: studies measured timed-up and go (TUG), femoral neck and spine bone mineral density (BMD)[21,32]. 1 study
L3916: analysed fibre type and muscle quality[31].
L3917: Of group 2 studies,[21,32,34,36] assessed lower limb strength, and[32,35] measured grip strength. Muscle
L3918: power was measured as sit-to-stand transfer power[33] and the stair climb test[34]. The short physical
L3919: performance battery (SPPB) was assessed by[32,34], and the TUG by[21,32,34]. BMD of the femoral
L3920: neck[21,32,34-36] and spine[21,32,34,35] were measured using DXA. Lean mass was measured using DXA[32
L3921: 34]and X-ray computed tomography (CT)[36]. Balance was assessed via the Romberg ratio[32], four square
L3922: step test[34], an AccuSway platform[35] and backwards walking[21]. Other outcomes included endurance (12
L3923: minute walk[32]), the 30 second sit-to-stand test[34], normal walking speed and the 5-time chair stand
L3924: test[21].
