L4139: **BMJ Open**
L4140: **GRADE analysis**
L4141: The GRADE summary of findings table for groups 1 and 2 are shown in Tables 5 and 6.
L4142: Within group 1, all studies were evaluated as moderate quality of evidence; no serious risk of bias was
L4143: detected. Due to the nature of the studies included within this review, no serious indirectness was detected;
L4144: all outcomes were measured directly without the use of a surrogate. Publication bias was not detected, and
L4145: due to the number of studies included, it was not possible to produce funnel plots for any outcomes. Although
L4146: publication bias was “not detected”, it is difficult to conclude that there was a complete absence of bias since
L4147: studies with significant results are more likely to be published than those reporting null or non-significant
L4148: results[25] Published, peer-reviewed articles were included in this review, since the Cochrane Handbook for
L4149: Systematic Reviews of Interventions further suggests that the inclusion of unpublished studies may introduce
L4150: additional bias, as these studies have not been strengthened by the peer-review process and may be of lower
L4151: methodological quality[25]. Reasons for downgrading the quality of evidence included serious inconsistency
L4152: due to substantial heterogeneity, and serious imprecision due to confidence intervals crossing the line of no
L4153: effect.
L4154: Within group 2 studies, 5 outcomes were graded as high to moderate quality of evidence (SPPB, TUG, muscle
L4155: strength of the lower limb, hand grip strength and BMD of the femoral neck). Remaining outcomes were
L4156: graded as low or very low quality, meaning that one could have little or very little confidence in the effect
L4157: estimate. Common reasons for downgrading outcomes included a combination of serious risk of bias (due to
L4158: the inclusion of study[34]), serious imprecision or serious inconsistency.
