L4353: **Results of individual studies and synthesis of results**
L4354: Results of the 2 groups of studies are reported separately. Qualitative syntheses were conducted for studies
L4355: with similar interventions and outcomes measures using RevMan 5.3 software. Study outcomes reporting
L4356: results in the same units were pooled using a fixed-effect meta-analysis. Effect sizes are expressed as
L4357: percentage mean differences or standardized mean differences (when outcomes were measured utilising
L4358: different methods), with 95% confidence intervals. Higher weighting was assigned to studies with smaller
L4359: standard deviations and a larger sample size[25]. Analyses were completed from extracted data; where
L4360: necessary data were estimated from statistics or figures, or requested from the authors of the article.
L4361: Heterogeneity was assessed via the chi squared test (Figures 2-14 and Tables 5-6). One article[36] was not
L4362: included in any of the quantitative analyses since the exercise intervention modality was considered to be too
L4363: dissimilar to compare with the other included articles. Within each group, there were outcomes unsuitable for
L4364: quantitative synthesis, due to a lack of studies with common outcomes or aspects of studies too dissimilar for
L4365: comparison; therefore, a narrative analysis was utilised.
L4366: **Quantitative synthesis**
L4367: Outcomes compared for group 1 included muscle strength of the lower limb, TUG and BMD of the femoral
L4368: neck and spine (Figures 2-5). Only muscle strength of the lower limb was found to be significant, with a large
L4369: effect size in favour of the intervention group (Figure 2. 2.69, 95% CI 0.95, 4.42. _p_ = 0.002).
L4370: Group 2 comparisons included the SPPB (Figure 6), TUG (Figure 7), muscle strength of the lower limb (Figure
L4371: 8), hand grip strength (Figure 9), weight (Figure 10), lean mass (Figure 11), fat mass (Figure 12), BMD of the
L4372: femoral neck (Figure 13) and spine (Figure 14). Of these outcomes, hand grip strength, weight, lean mass, fat
L4373: mass and the BMD of the spine were found to be non-significant. However, SPPB score was more improved in
L4374: the intervention group (1.09, 95% CI 0.15, 2.03. _p_ = 0.02), with a significant and large effect. Similarly, TUG was
L4375: significantly reduced within the intervention group (-1.57, 95% CI -2.50, -0.64. _p_ = 0.0010). The results of the
L4376: quantitative analysis also supported the combined intervention for muscle strength of the lower limb (2.69,
L4377: 95% CI 0.95, 4.42). _p_ = 0.002), and BMD of the femoral neck (0.04, 95% CI 0.01, 0.06. _p_ = 0.002).
L4378: **Qualitative synthesis**
L4379: Referring to the narrative synthesis guidelines provided by the Cochrane Consumers and Communication
L4380: Review Group[38], it was appropriate to apply 2 steps listed; developing a preliminary synthesis and exploring
L4381: the relationships within and between studies. To develop a primary synthesis, results were systematically
L4382: tabulated to identify patterns across studies (Tables 7-9). Exploring the relationships between and within
L4383: studies for group 1, the control group in study[31]demonstrated a significant percentage increase in CSA of the
L4384: quadriceps from baseline in comparison to the intervention group (+8.46% versus +4.94%, _p_ < 0.05).
