L4453: Group 1 studies compared vitamin D3 supplementation and exercise training versus exercise alone
L4454: Comparing primary outcomes for group 2, the percentage increase in isometric knee extensor strength for
L4455: study[36] was greater in the intervention group (+3.01% versus +0.11%), although not statistically significant.
L4456: Muscle power was compared in studies[33] and[34], expressed as sit-to-stand transfer power and functional
L4457: stair climbing muscle power respectively. Both studies reported a significant percentage increase in muscle
L4458: power within the intervention groups, and smaller, non-significant increases within the control groups (sit-to
L4459: stand transfer power intervention group +8.00% versus +2.61%, _p_ = 0.017; functional stair climbing muscle
L4460: power intervention group +10.51% versus +7.32%, _p_ < 0.05).
L4461: The 30 second sit-to-stand test showed significant favourable results for the combined intervention of exercise
L4462: and vitamin D3 (+10.40% versus +6.20%, p<0.05). Within study[21], normal walking speed and the 5-time chair
L4463: stand time deteriorated non-significantly in both groups. The 12-minute walk test in study[32] was further
L4464: improved within the control group, although this did not achieve statistical significance. The four-square step
L4465: test, body sway and backwards walking were significantly more improved in the intervention groups. Only
L4466: Romberg ratio showed the greatest improvement within the control group; Romberg ratio was decreased in
L4467: comparison with the intervention group, although the results were non-significant (+2.8% versus -0.60%).
