L4618: Group 2 compared vitamin D3 supplementation and exercise training versus vitamin D3 supplementation alone
L4619: In summary, meta-analyses for group 1 found muscle strength of the lower limb to be significantly improved
L4620: within the intervention group (0.98, 95% CI 0.73, 1.24, _p_ <0.001). All other outcomes showed small but non
L4621: significant positive effects for the intervention group. The SPPB, TUG, muscle strength of the lower limb and
L4622: femoral neck BMD all showed significantly greater improvements in the intervention group for group 2
L4623: comparisons.
L4624: The narrative analysis revealed significant differences in body composition, muscle power, muscle function
L4625: and balance. A significant percentage increase in quadriceps CSA was observed in the control group of
L4626: study[31]. The combined intervention of RET and vitamin D3 supplementation resulted in a greater percentage
L4627: increase in muscle strength and power, and a greater improvement in the 30 second sit-to-stand test, the four
L4628: square step test, body sway and backwards walking. However, vitamin D3 supplementation alone resulted in a
L4629: greater improvement in the 12-minute walk test and Romberg ratio.
