L4700: **BMJ Open**
L4701: **DISCUSSION**
L4702: The aim of this systematic review was to assess the combined effect of RET and vitamin D3 supplementation
L4703: on musculoskeletal health in older adults. Only 7 studies were eligible for inclusion, with a total of 792
L4704: participants, highlighting the lack of available literature on the topic. Studies were categorised into 2 groups;
L4705: studies in which all participants took part in RET and the intervention group was supplemented with vitamin
L4706: D3, or studies in which all participants were supplemented with vitamin D3 and the intervention group took
L4707: part in RET. 2 studies were categorized into both group 1 and group 2.
L4708: **Quantitative analysis**
L4709: Data analysis conducted for this review included meta-analyses and narrative reviews. Meta-analyses for
L4710: group 1 included muscle strength of the lower limb, TUG and BMD of both the femoral neck and spine.
L4711: Evidence of additional benefit was shown for all outcomes within the intervention group; however, the effect
L4712: size was small and non-significant for TUG and BMD of the femoral neck and spine. Muscle strength of the
L4713: lower limb was the only significant outcome of group 1, with a large effect size observed within the
L4714: intervention group (0.98, 95% CI 0.73, 1.24. _p_ <0.00001). Although numerous studies have demonstrated the
L4715: beneficial effect of RET on muscle strength in older adults[3-5], this result provides evidence that vitamin D3
L4716: supplementation may enhance these effects in older adults. Skeletal muscle myopathies associated with
L4717: vitamin D deficiency are well documented[39], and symptoms of significant muscle weakness are reversed
L4718: with treatment of the deficiency[40]. A systematic review and meta-analysis reported a gain in lower extremity
L4719: strength with vitamin D supplementation only in vitamin D deficient older adults; no effect was observed in
L4720: replete adults[22]. Similarly, no effect of vitamin D3 supplementation on isometric quadriceps strength was
L4721: demonstrated after 6 months in vitamin D replete older adults[41]. Interestingly, although the studies included
L4722: within group 1[21,31,32] did not specify serum 25(OH)D levels as inclusion/exclusion criteria, baseline and
L4723: post-intervention serum 25(OH)D were within the ‘sufficient’ range (>30nmol.L [-1] ). A greater increase of muscle
L4724: strength in replete older adults represents a novel finding of this review. Preliminary support for combined
L4725: vitamin D supplementation and RET was demonstrated in a 3-month longitudinal study examining the effect of
L4726: serum 25(OH)D and exercise training on functional performance in older men and women aged 65 years and
L4727: over. No significant improvements in function were reported in participants with lower serum 25(OH)D (<47.5
L4728: nmol.L [-1] ), however higher serum 25(OH)D (>67.5 nmol.L [-1] ) was associated with greatest improvements in
L4729: functionality and muscle strength[42].
L4730: This finding must be considered within the context of the risk of bias and GRADE analyses. The risk of bias
L4731: analysis showed an overall unclear risk of bias for the included studies, and the GRADE analysis concluded that
L4732: the evidenced was of moderate quality; however, serious inconsistency due to moderate heterogeneity (I [2] =
L4733: 70%) was detected. This heterogeneity may have been due to the differing duration of interventions (12 weeks
L4734: to 24 months), differences between measurement methodologies, differences between exercise regimens
