L4799: (although all adopted progressive RET), doses of vitamin D3 (400 IU to 1920 IU per day), or may indicate that
L4800: these studies were unsuitable for comparison.
L4801: Significant effects for the SPPB, TUG, muscle strength of the lower limb, and the BMD of the femoral neck
L4802: were observed within the intervention groups of group 2 studies; unsurprisingly, RET was found to have a
L4803: positive influence. In a recent systematic review and meta-analysis, exercise significantly increased SPPB score
L4804: and decreased TUG time, with large effect sizes (1.87 and -2.47 respectively[43]); similar results are reported
L4805: within this review. Vitamin D is a regulator of BMD, proliferating calcium and phosphate absorption in the
L4806: intestine and acting directly on bone cells[44]. Vitamin D has previously been shown to influence BMD,
L4807: fracture rate and risk[45]; studies of patients who have sustained a hip fracture typically demonstrated low
L4808: serum vitamin D (≤30.0 nmol.L [-1] ;[46]). Supplementation of vitamin D and calcium has been shown to
L4809: significantly decrease the rate of bone loss in the hip and spine[47]. GRADE analyses for these outcomes
L4810: concluded the quality of evidence to be high (SPPB and TUG) or moderate (muscle strength of the lower limb
L4811: and BMD of the femoral neck).
L4812: Closer examination of the control groups within significant outcomes for group 2 was undertaken to evaluate
L4813: the effect of vitamin D3 supplementation alone. Intriguingly, although the intervention groups (RET and
L4814: vitamin D3 supplementation) showed evidence of benefit in number of outcomes, the control groups (vitamin
L4815: D3 supplementation alone) showed mixed, or even negative impacts on the same outcomes. SPPB score was
L4816: decreased post-intervention compared with baseline by 0.30% and 0.50% in the control groups of studies[32]
L4817: and[33] respectively. Muscle strength of the lower limb and BMD of the femoral neck showed mixed results
L4818: for the intervention groups, with some studies reporting small increases and others reporting small losses
L4819: (non-significant). Previous reports of the effect of vitamin D supplementation on muscle strength and physical
L4820: functioning are mixed; the InCHIANTI study of people aged 65 years or over reported a significant association
L4821: between serum 25(OH)D <25nmol.L [-1] and SPPB score[48]. Similarly, a large prospective cohort of older adults
L4822: aged 65 years or over found those with low (<25nmol.L [-1] ) 25(OH)D were significantly more likely to experience
L4823: losses in grip strength and higher rates of appendicular lean mass loss compared to those with higher (>50
L4824: nmol.L [-1] ) 25(OH)D[23]. Conversely, another large, prospective study found no association between serum
L4825: 25(OH)D, walking speed and time for repeated chair stands[49]. The TUG test time increased in all groups of
L4826: study [32], and was significantly increased in the vitamin D without exercise group in study p=0.01) [21]. Again,
L4827: participants included in studies[32] and [21] had sufficient serum 25(OH)D levels, indicating that
L4828: supplementation in replete older adults may not confer additional benefits to neuromuscular function unless
L4829: combined with exercise.
