L4893: **BMJ Open**
L4894: **Narrative analysis**
L4895: Studies in group 1[21,31,32] had few body composition outcomes in common, therefore a narrative analysis
L4896: was conducted. The CSA of the quadriceps was analysed within study[31], and results showed that although
L4897: the intervention group did experience a +4.94%, increase from baseline, the control group (not supplemented
L4898: with vitamin D3) actually showed a significantly higher increase in quadriceps CSA (+8.46%, _p_ <0.05).
L4899: These results do not provide evidence for the additive effects of combined exercise training and vitamin D3.
L4900: Other study groups have reported changes in muscle CSA consequent to RET which are both smaller[8,50] and
L4901: comparable[51] to those reported in study[31]. Interestingly, study[31] also assessed “muscle quality” (muscle
L4902: strength/CSA); although non-significant, the intervention group improved their muscle quality to a greater
L4903: degree than the control group (+9.61% versus +0.66% change from baseline), f indicating an increased
L4904: functionality of the muscle to produce force; conceptually more relevant in combatting the effects of
L4905: sarcopenia than muscle size and strength alone[52].
L4906: Results of the narrative analysis for group 2 showed that the combined intervention of RET and vitamin D3
L4907: supplementation was significantly more beneficial than vitamin D3 supplementation alone for sit-to-stand
L4908: transfer power, functional stair climbing muscle power, 30 second sit-to-stand, 5-time chair stand, the four
L4909: square step test, body sway and backwards walking. Only body sway was negatively affected by vitamin D3
L4910: supplementation, although the within group change was non-significant. Other outcomes of interest included
L4911: normal walking speed, which deteriorated in both groups, the distance walked in 12 minutes and Romberg
L4912: ratio, in which the control groups made the most improvement, although not significantly.
L4913: **Limitations**
L4914: Few published studies were eligible for inclusion within this review, although this serves to highlight the
L4915: knowledge gap with respect to this topic. The inclusion of a high-risk study was deemed necessary due to the
L4916: lack of available literature, although this had a negative effect on the perceived quality of evidence for the
L4917: outcomes in which it was reported. Generally, outcome measure data could be graded as representing
L4918: moderate quality, although there were several outcome measures graded as low or very low quality, due to
L4919: the high variability of participant numbers, duration of interventions, exercise methodologies or differing
L4920: vitamin D3 doses and period of supplementation employed within the studies. Furthermore, data produced
L4921: from meta-analyses including study[21] may have been skewed due to the high weighting assigned for this
L4922: study as a result of the large number of participants recruited.
L4923: Of the individual studies included within this review, none reported inclusion/exclusion criterion for vitamin D
L4924: status, and although at baseline serum vitamin D was not significantly different between the groups in 5
L4925: studies[21,31-33,36], 2 studies reported no data for serum vitamin D pre or post-intervention[34,35].
L4926: Additionally, analysis methods used within 5 studies included did not account for confounding factors[31
L4927: 34,36], and participants were not stratified on the basis of any characteristics in 3 studies[21,31,35], although
