L4991: **BMJ Open**
L4992: these were single-sex studies. Unfortunately, several outcome measures were unsuitable for inclusion within
L4993: the qualitative analysis due to differing measurement methodologies utilised or too few outcome measures in
L4994: common. A recent systematic review and meta-analysis investigating the effects of vitamin D on
L4995: neuromuscular remodelling following exercise or injury similarly found few eligible studies and high levels of
L4996: heterogeneity due to methodological differences, resulting in the authors to suggest more high quality
L4997: evidence is needed to reach a result that is conclusive [53].
L4998: **CONCLUSION**
L4999: This review provides tentative support for the additive effect of combined RET and vitamin D3
L5000: supplementation for the improvement of muscle strength in older adults. For other aspects of musculoskeletal
L5001: function, such as SPPB and TUG, no additional benefit beyond that gained from exercise training was found.
L5002: This review showed no evidence of benefit of vitamin D3 supplementation alone, however, few studies were
L5003: identified during the literature search, highlighting that further evidence is required to draw any firm
L5004: conclusions or make explicit recommendations regarding vitamin D3 supplementation for musculoskeletal
L5005: health and function in older adults.
L5006: Our recommendations to enable future studies to definitively answer questions regarding the additive effects
L5007: of the combined vitamin D3 supplementation and RET include; common outcomes relevant to the condition
L5008: studied, for example the SPPB, 400m walk and gait speed are recommended to assess physical
L5009: performance[54], which would allow for a more detailed assessment of results. Additionally, exercise
L5010: interventions of similar durations would allow for a more accurate comparison between studies; it has been
L5011: suggested that interventions with older adults should be of a minimum duration of 3 months to obtain
L5012: significant differences in relevant outcomes [54]. Reporting of confounding factors would allow for adjustment
L5013: of results via the use of covariates; for example, objective measures of physical activity using accelerometers,
L5014: baseline serum vitamin D3 status and participant characteristics, which may bias the participant pool. Separate
L5015: analysis of male and female participants, or the addition of sex as a covariate in any analysis models would
L5016: help to address sex-related differences in performance. Regarding study design, four-armed RCT studies are
L5017: best placed to answer combined effects research questions; i.e. exercise intervention, vitamin D intervention,
L5018: both exercise and vitamin D, neither exercise nor vitamin D (true control). A true control group was lacking
L5019: from a number of the included studies within this review.
